import{c as r,$ as i,Q as s,j as o,g as t,o as n,n as l,m as d}from"./chunks/framework.Ce_49mA8.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Analytics & Reports","description":"End-to-end financial analytics, revenue and expense trends, break-even point analysis, and master labor ROI in OmniCraft ERP.","frontmatter":{"title":"Analytics & Reports","description":"End-to-end financial analytics, revenue and expense trends, break-even point analysis, and master labor ROI in OmniCraft ERP.","datePublished":"2026-08-20","dateModified":"2026-11-24","order":5},"headers":[],"relativePath":"docs/resources/analytics.md","filePath":"docs/resources/analytics.md","lastUpdated":1795478400000}'),c={name:"docs/resources/analytics.md"},h={id:"analytics-financial-charts",tabindex:"-1"};function u(p,e,m,g,f,y){const a=i("DocIcon");return s(),o("div",null,[t("h1",h,[n(a,{name:"analytics"}),e[0]||(e[0]=l(" Analytics & Financial Charts ",-1)),e[1]||(e[1]=t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#analytics-financial-charts","aria-label":'Permalink to "Analytics & Financial Charts"'},"​",-1))]),e[2]||(e[2]=d('<p>The <strong>&quot;Analytics&quot;</strong> tab serves as the command center and interactive dashboard for your operations. While Cash Flow (DDS) controls immediate liquid funds, Warehouse and Workshop track physical inventory, and the Capital report reflects total business valuation, Analytics answers the owner&#39;s primary strategic question:</p><blockquote><p><strong>&quot;How sustainably is my business growing, and what real financial return does each hour of craft labor generate?&quot;</strong></p></blockquote><p>OmniCraft ERP automatically consolidates data across all operational modules — sales logs, inventory valuation, completed service work orders, and cash transactions. It translates these figures into key performance metrics and visual trend lines.</p><hr><h2 id="three-core-business-health-indicators" tabindex="-1">Three Core Business Health Indicators <a class="header-anchor" href="#three-core-business-health-indicators" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Three Core Business Health Indicators&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The top panel presents three real-time indicator cards calculated automatically from your full transaction history.</p><h3 id="_1-average-ticket" tabindex="-1">1. Average Ticket <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-average-ticket" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Average Ticket&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Displays the average gross revenue generated per completed deal — whether a retail customer sale or a fulfilled service order.</p><ul><li><strong>Why track it:</strong> It directly reflects the effectiveness of your pricing strategy, product bundling, and cross-selling.</li><li><strong>Practical insight:</strong> If you introduced premium packaging, created gift bundles, or updated your service rates, a rising average ticket proves that your customers perceive higher value.</li></ul><h3 id="_2-break-even-point" tabindex="-1">2. Break-Even Point <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-break-even-point" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Break-Even Point&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A fundamental threshold for operational sustainability. The system analyzes your fixed and variable overhead expenses over the preceding six months (rent, software, utilities, marketing, and linear equipment depreciation) and divides this figure by the average gross profit per sale.</p><ul><li><strong>Metric interpretation:</strong> The number indicates the <strong>minimum monthly volume of items or orders</strong> required to cover all operating expenses without drawing on personal reserves.</li><li><strong>Economic benchmark:</strong> Every unit produced and sold beyond this threshold generates pure net profit for the owner.</li></ul><h3 id="_3-master-labor-return-time-roi" tabindex="-1">3. Master Labor Return (Time ROI) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-master-labor-return-time-roi" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Master Labor Return (Time ROI)&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A specialized metric engineered specifically for craftspeople, makers, and service engineers.</p><div class="formula-hero-card"><div class="formula-card-top"><span class="formula-badge">Economic Model</span><div class="formula-heading">Craftsman Labor Return (Time ROI)</div></div><div class="formula-equation-box"><div class="math-line"><span class="math-var">Time ROI ($/hour)</span><span class="math-op">=</span><span class="math-fraction"><span class="math-numerator">Total Net Profit</span><span class="math-denominator">Accumulated Labor Hours</span></span></div></div><div class="formula-card-bottom"><p>Reveals authentic hourly return at the workstation, machine, or order desk after deducting material consumption, workshop overhead, and depreciation.</p></div></div><ul><li><strong>Metric interpretation:</strong> The net financial output generated by <strong>one hour</strong> spent at the craftsman&#39;s workplace or repair station.</li><li><strong>Practical insight:</strong> This calculation eliminates guesswork and uncovers labor-draining traps. A complex bespoke showpiece with a high price tag might yield only $8/hour due to painstaking assembly, while a streamlined standard item yields $45/hour. This provides a data-driven justification to update price tags or re-engineer labor-heavy designs.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="financial-pulse-six-month-trend-analysis" tabindex="-1">Financial Pulse: Six-Month Trend Analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#financial-pulse-six-month-trend-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Financial Pulse: Six-Month Trend Analysis&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The multi-line chart visualizes your financial evolution over the trailing six months, integrating three key indicators:</p><ul><li>🟢 <strong>Revenue:</strong> Total cash inflow received from customer transactions.</li><li>🟠 <strong>Total Expenses:</strong> The true cost of goods sold based on moving average cost (MAC) plus overhead expenses recorded in Cash Flow and linear equipment depreciation from the Workshop tab.</li><li>🔵 <strong>Net Profit:</strong> Real operational profit (the monthly surplus between revenue and total expenses).</li></ul><div class="tip custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Beware the &quot;Financial Scissors&quot; Trap</p><p>Keep a close watch on the gap between the green revenue line and the orange expense line. If revenue is steadily expanding while the profit line stays flat or declines, your profit margin is deteriorating.</p><p>This signals rising supplier costs, increased delivery expenses, or unchecked overhead. When this occurs, review material purchase costs in the Warehouse and adjust production formulas in the Calculator.</p></div><hr><h2 id="revenue-structure-sales-drivers-abc-ranking" tabindex="-1">Revenue Structure: Sales Drivers &amp; ABC Ranking <a class="header-anchor" href="#revenue-structure-sales-drivers-abc-ranking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Revenue Structure: Sales Drivers &amp; ABC Ranking&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The pie chart highlights which products or services generate the bulk of your cash flow.</p><p>The system automatically ranks catalog performance, spotlighting the <strong>top five revenue drivers</strong> and grouping remaining items into the &quot;Others&quot; category.</p><table class="craft-matrix-table"><thead><tr><th style="width:40%;">Catalog Segment</th><th style="width:60%;">Cash Flow Contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Top 5 Leader Group</strong></td><td>Revenue drivers: generate 70–85% of total operating cash flow and net margin.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>&quot;Others&quot; Segment</strong></td><td>Catalog variety, bespoke experimental projects, and auxiliary accessories.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="strategic-takeaways" tabindex="-1">Strategic Takeaways: <a class="header-anchor" href="#strategic-takeaways" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Strategic Takeaways:&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Protect Your Flagships:</strong> Top-tier items must never run out of stock. Prioritize raw material purchasing and stock allocation for these items above all else.</li><li><strong>Trim Low-Yield Clutter:</strong> Items generating negligible revenue deserve scrutiny. If a low-selling item requires rare supplies or tricky machine calibration, consider pruning it from your standard catalog.</li></ol><hr><h2 id="workshop-decision-matrix" tabindex="-1">Workshop Decision Matrix <a class="header-anchor" href="#workshop-decision-matrix" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Workshop Decision Matrix&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The true power of analytics emerges when cross-examining multiple indicators:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Screen Observations</th><th style="text-align:left;">Diagnosis</th><th style="text-align:left;">Recommended Workshop Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Low Time ROI</strong> despite a high average ticket</td><td style="text-align:left;">Labor-intensive production draining work hours</td><td style="text-align:left;">Recalibrate labor estimates, raise prices in the Calculator, or simplify product assemblies</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Break-Even threshold rising</strong> month over month</td><td style="text-align:left;">Fixed overhead expenses expanding unchecked</td><td style="text-align:left;">Audit Cash Flow (DDS), inspect recurring subscriptions, and reduce unnecessary costs</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Revenue depends heavily</strong> on a single item (&gt;75%)</td><td style="text-align:left;">Vulnerability to shifts in market demand or supplier delays</td><td style="text-align:left;">Diversify the catalog and develop complementary variations</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><strong>Revenue climbs</strong> while net profit stagnates</td><td style="text-align:left;">The scissors effect: accelerating material costs eroding margin</td><td style="text-align:left;">Inspect weighted average costs in the Warehouse and update logistics fees in receipt records</td></tr></tbody></table><hr><h2 id="empty-state-guided-demo-mode" tabindex="-1">Empty State &amp; Guided Demo Mode <a class="header-anchor" href="#empty-state-guided-demo-mode" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Empty State &amp; Guided Demo Mode&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When opening the tab in a fresh workspace, Analytics greets you with a clean onboarding panel:</p><ul><li><strong>Go to Operations:</strong> One-click navigation to Sales or Service Orders to record your first transactions.</li><li><strong>&quot;Demo Mode&quot; Button:</strong> Populates the view with realistic simulated workshop data. This allows you to explore chart behavior, test interactive tooltips, and understand reporting logic before entering your own production history.</li></ul><hr><h2 id="troubleshooting-common-pitfalls" tabindex="-1">Troubleshooting &amp; Common Pitfalls <a class="header-anchor" href="#troubleshooting-common-pitfalls" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Troubleshooting &amp; Common Pitfalls&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="faq-grid"><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">What should I do if analytics charts and KPI cards appear empty?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>In a fresh workspace with no completed transactions, analytics remains in a clean waiting state. Record at least one sale through the POS terminal or activate &quot;Demo Mode&quot; to explore analytical capabilities using simulated data.</p></div></details><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">Why does Time ROI display as &quot;N/A&quot; or zero?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Hourly return on time is calculated from tracked labor hours. Open the recipe in the Calculator and verify the &quot;Labor Time (hrs)&quot; field: if labor is set to zero, the engine cannot allocate margin across working hours.</p></div></details><details class="faq-item"><summary class="faq-summary">How do I deactivate demonstration data and return to authentic metrics?</summary><div class="faq-content"><p>Click the Demo Mode toggle in the upper right corner of the view. The interface instantly removes the simulated overlay and restores actual database records.</p></div></details></div>',39))])}const k=r(c,[["render",u]]);export{b as __pageData,k as default};
